# Summary {#summary}

This report analyses the six-bar linkage of the MIET1077 major assignment: a crank (link 2) drives a ternary coupler (link 3) that is held by a rocker (link 4); a connecting rod (link 5) taken from point C of the coupler drives a slider (link 6) on a horizontal guide. The crank length is OA = <<n(p.OA*1000,0)>> mm and it turns anticlockwise at a constant <<n(p.n2_rpm,0)>> rpm (ω₂ = <<n(p.omega2,3)>> rad/s). All other lengths follow from the course data-selection rules (OE = CD = 4·OA, AC = 3/5·AB, H = OA/4) with AB = <<n(p.AB*1000,0)>> mm and BE = <<n(p.BE*1000,0)>> mm chosen to give a Grashof crank–rocker with a good transmission angle (never below <<n(R.transmission.fourbar_min,1)>>°).

**What the mechanism does.** The crank turns fully, the rocker swings through <<n(rk_swing,1)>>°, and the slider makes a <<n(stroke*1000,1)>> mm stroke between S = <<n(S_min*1000,1)>> mm and <<n(S_max*1000,1)>> mm from the crank pivot. The slider's rightward stroke takes <<n(ang_fwd,1)>>° of crank rotation and its return takes <<n(ang_ret,1)>>°, a quick-return time ratio of <<n(Q,2)>>.

