**Agreement between methods.** The graphical polygons, read with ruler precision (±0.5 mm), agree with the analytical vector-loop solution to within <<n(max_dg_vel,1)>> % for velocities and <<n(max_dg_acc,1)>> % for accelerations. The instantaneous-centre method agrees to within <<n(max_dic,1)>> %; its largest error comes from reading a short (≈15 mm) radius at 1:10 scale. An independent multibody simulation, built the way Working Model builds a model (bodies, pins, slot, velocity motor) and integrated in time, reproduces every analytical quantity, including all pin forces and the motor torque, to better than 10⁻⁷ over the whole revolution.

**Recommendations.**

- **Balance:** fit counterweights on the crank and the rocker. They cut the RMS shaking force by <<n(-pct(s8.stats.berkof_lowen.Fs_rms, s8.stats.unbalanced.Fs_rms),0)>> % (exact Berkof–Lowen solution, about <<n(s8.designs.berkof_lowen.m2c + s8.designs.berkof_lowen.m4c,1)>> kg at 120 mm radius) or <<n(-pct(s8.stats.optimal_lsq.Fs_rms, s8.stats.unbalanced.Fs_rms),0)>> % (least-squares optimum). The slider's reciprocating inertia cannot be cancelled by rotating weights.
- **Drive:** the motor sees ±<<n(s7.summary.T12_max,0)>> N·m peaks with zero mean; specify a flywheel so the speed stays constant, especially once counterweights are added.
- **Bearings:** rate the crank bearing O and pin A for ≈ <<n(s7.summary.pin_max.F12/1000,1)>> kN and ≈ <<n(s7.summary.pin_max.F32/1000,1)>> kN peak dynamic load (plus the usual safety factor).

# Objectives {#objectives}

- Select the dimensions of the linkage from the given data using the course data-selection (non-equality) equations and check mobility, the Grashof condition and the transmission angle.
- Determine all positions of the links and joints graphically for 16 crank positions, the joint paths and the limits of motion.
- Determine linear and angular velocities and accelerations at θ₂ = 60° graphically (vector polygons drawn to scale).
- Locate all 15 instantaneous centres with Kennedy's theorem and use them to find the velocities of A, B, C and D.
- Derive and solve the analytical (vector loop, complex number) equations for position, velocity and acceleration of all five moving links, and compare them with the graphical results.
- Set up and solve the 14 × 14 matrix of dynamic joint forces and the driving torque at θ₂ = 60°, from free-body diagrams of every link.
- Build a computational model of the mechanism and use it to compute paths, velocities, accelerations, pin forces, slider side load and driving torque over one revolution, and compare it with the other methods.
- Determine the shaking force and shaking moment and develop an analytical strategy for balancing the mechanism.

# Related Theory {#theory}

**Mechanism, mobility and Grashof's criterion.** A mechanism is an assembly of rigid links joined by kinematic pairs that transmits a constrained motion (Week 1; Subic & Alam 2010). The degree of freedom of a planar mechanism follows from the Gruebler–Kutzbach equation,
$$\text{DOF} = 3(n-1) - 2P_1 - P_2 = 3(6-1) - 2(7) - 0 = 1,$$
with n = 6 links (including the frame), P₁ = 7 full joints (pins O, A, B, C, D, E and the slider pair) and P₂ = 0 half joints. One input, the crank angle θ₂, therefore fixes the configuration. Grashof's criterion, s + l ≤ p + q, guarantees that the shortest link of a four-bar can rotate fully (Week 1). When the shortest link is the crank next to the frame, the four-bar O-A-B-E is a crank–rocker.

**Types of motion.** Link 2 rotates about a fixed axis, link 4 oscillates about a fixed axis, links 3 and 5 have general plane motion, and the slider (link 6) translates rectilinearly (Supplementary Guide, Figure 1).

**Relative-motion (graphical) method.** For two points A and B of the same rigid link,
$$\mathbf V_B = \mathbf V_A + \mathbf V_{B/A},\qquad |\mathbf V_{B/A}| = \omega\, r_{AB},\quad \mathbf V_{B/A}\perp AB,$$
$$\mathbf a_B = \mathbf a_A + \mathbf a^n_{B/A} + \mathbf a^t_{B/A},$$
$$a^n_{B/A} = \omega^2 r_{AB} = \frac{V_{B/A}^2}{r_{AB}}\ (\text{directed from B towards A}),\qquad a^t_{B/A} = \alpha\, r_{AB}\ (\perp AB).$$
Absolute vectors start from the pole of the polygon. Vectors of points on ground-connected links start from the pole. Every normal component must be calculated before an acceleration polygon can be closed (Week 3). The polygon points of one link form an *image* similar to the link, which gives the velocity and acceleration of any further point, such as a mass centre.

**Instantaneous centres.** An instantaneous centre (IC) is the point common to two links at which they have no relative velocity. A mechanism of n links has $C = n(n-1)/2$ ICs. Pins are permanent ICs, and the IC of a slider and its straight guide lies at infinity perpendicular to the guide. The *Aronhold–Kennedy theorem* states that the three ICs of any three links lie on one straight line, which locates the remaining ICs (Week 5). The velocity of any point is then $V = \omega\, r_{IC}$, perpendicular to the radius from the IC.

**Analytical (complex-number vector-loop) method.** Every link is a vector $\mathbf R = l\,e^{j\theta} = l(\cos\theta + j\sin\theta)$ (Euler's identity). Writing each closed loop as a vector sum equal to zero and separating real (x) and imaginary (y) parts gives the position equations. Differentiating once, with $\tfrac{d}{dt}e^{j\theta} = j\omega e^{j\theta}$, gives the velocities, and differentiating again gives the accelerations, with $\tfrac{d}{dt}(j\omega e^{j\theta}) = (j\alpha - \omega^2)e^{j\theta}$ (Week 7).

**Dynamic force analysis (matrix method).** Newton–Euler equations for each rigid link, $\sum \mathbf F = m\mathbf a_G$ and $\sum M_G = I_G\alpha$ (or D'Alembert's form $\sum\mathbf F - m\mathbf a_G = 0$), written for every moving link, give a linear system $[A]\{B\} = \{C\}$. Here [A] holds the geometry (unit coefficients and the R-vector components), {B} the unknown joint forces and the driving torque, and {C} the inertia terms (Week 9).

**Shaking force, shaking moment and balancing.** The shaking force is the vector sum of all forces the moving links apply to the frame, $\mathbf F_s = \mathbf F_{21}+\mathbf F_{41}+\mathbf F_{61} = -\sum m_i\mathbf a_{Gi}$. The shaking moment is the reaction torque plus the moments of these ground forces (Week 10). A linkage is completely force-balanced when the total centre of mass of its moving links is stationary. Berkof & Lowen (1969) showed how to achieve this for a four-bar with counterweights on the two ground-pivoted links. Rotating-mass balancing uses $\sum m r = 0$ (static) and $\sum m r a = 0$ (dynamic) (Week 10). Reciprocating masses can only be partially balanced by rotating counterweights.

**Computational multibody method.** Programs such as Working Model 2D describe each body by the coordinates (x, y, φ) of its mass centre, the joints by algebraic constraint equations Φ(q, t) = 0 and a motor by a driving constraint. They then solve $M\ddot q + \Phi_q^T\lambda = Q$ at every time step. The Lagrange multipliers λ are the joint reaction forces and the motor torque (Uicker et al. 2017; Norton 2012).

