# 5. Analytical Solution — Vector Loops and Complex Numbers {#sec5}

The course template "Approach for the analytical part of the major assignment" (Week 7) splits the mechanism into Part 1 (crank, point A), Part 2 (coupler and rocker, points B and C) and Part 3 (slider, point D).

**Vectors.** $\mathbf R_2 = l_2e^{j\theta_2}$ (O→A), $\mathbf R_3 = l_3e^{j\theta_3}$ (A→B), $\mathbf R_4 = l_4e^{j\theta_4}$ (E→B), $\mathbf R_1 = l_1e^{j\theta_1}$ (O→E), $\mathbf R_{AC} = \overline{AC}\,e^{j(\theta_3-\beta)}$ (A→C; C lies below AB), $\mathbf R_5 = l_5e^{j\theta_5}$ (C→D) and $\mathbf R_D = S - jH$ (O→D).

$$\text{Loop 1 (O-A-B-E):}\quad l_2e^{j\theta_2} + l_3e^{j\theta_3} = l_1e^{j\theta_1} + l_4e^{j\theta_4}$$
$$\text{Loop 2 (O-A-C-D):}\quad l_2e^{j\theta_2} + \overline{AC}\,e^{j(\theta_3-\beta)} + l_5e^{j\theta_5} = S - jH$$

## 5.1 Position analysis

**Part 1 – crank (point A).**
$$x_A = l_2\cos\theta_2 = <<n(p.OA*1000,0)>>\cos60^\circ = <<n(d.pos_A[0]*1000,2)>>\ \text{mm},\qquad y_A = l_2\sin\theta_2 = <<n(d.pos_A[1]*1000,2)>>\ \text{mm}.$$

**Part 2 – coupler and rocker (points B and C).** Following the template, let $\mathbf R_{AE} = \mathbf R_1 - \mathbf R_2 = \text{Re} + j\,\text{Im}$:
$$\text{Re} = l_1\cos\theta_1 - l_2\cos\theta_2 = <<n(p.OE,3)>>\cos<<n(p.theta1_deg,0)>>^\circ - <<n(p.OA,3)>>\cos60^\circ = <<n(d.template.Re,5)>>\ \text{m}$$
$$\text{Im} = l_1\sin\theta_1 - l_2\sin\theta_2 = <<n(p.OE,3)>>\sin<<n(p.theta1_deg,0)>>^\circ - <<n(p.OA,3)>>\sin60^\circ = <<n(d.template.Im,5)>>\ \text{m}$$
Loop 1 becomes $l_3e^{j\theta_3} = \text{Re} + j\text{Im} + l_4e^{j\theta_4}$. Its real and imaginary parts are
$$l_3\cos\theta_3 = \text{Re} + l_4\cos\theta_4,\qquad l_3\sin\theta_3 = \text{Im} + l_4\sin\theta_4 .$$
Squaring and adding eliminates θ₃:
$$l_3^2 = \text{Re}^2 + \text{Im}^2 + l_4^2 + 2l_4(\text{Re}\cos\theta_4 + \text{Im}\sin\theta_4)\quad\Rightarrow$$
$$\text{Re}\cos\theta_4 + \text{Im}\sin\theta_4 + C = 0,\qquad C = \frac{\text{Re}^2+\text{Im}^2+l_4^2-l_3^2}{2l_4} = <<n(d.template.C,5)>>\ \text{m}.$$
With $t = \tan(\theta_4/2)$, $\sin\theta_4 = \dfrac{2t}{1+t^2}$ and $\cos\theta_4 = \dfrac{1-t^2}{1+t^2}$; multiplying through by $(1+t^2)$:
$$\text{Re}\,(1-t^2) + 2\,\text{Im}\,t + C\,(1+t^2) = 0\quad\Rightarrow\quad (C-\text{Re})\,t^2 + 2\,\text{Im}\,t + (C+\text{Re}) = 0,$$
$$t = \frac{-\text{Im}\pm\sqrt{\text{Re}^2+\text{Im}^2-C^2}}{C-\text{Re}} = \frac{<<n(-d.template.Im,5)>> \pm <<n(d.template.sqrt,5)>>}{<<n(d.template.C - d.template.Re,5)>>}.$$
The two roots are $t = <<n(d.template.t_minus,4)>>$ (θ₄ = <<n(d.template.th4_minus,3)>>°) and $t = <<n(d.template.t_plus,4)>>$ (θ₄ = <<n(d.template.th4_plus,3)>>°). The template takes the solution with 0 < θ₄ < π, which is the open configuration of the handout figure (B above E):
$$\theta_4 = <<n(d.theta4,3)>>^\circ\quad(\text{the “−” sign here}).$$
θ₃ follows from the two component equations above and the half-angle identity $\tan(\theta_3/2) = \sin\theta_3/(1+\cos\theta_3)$, which is the template's triangle AB′B result:
$$\theta_3 = 2\arctan\frac{\text{Im} + l_4\sin\theta_4}{l_3 + \text{Re} + l_4\cos\theta_4} = 2\arctan\frac{<<n(d.template.Im + p.BE*math.sin(math.radians(d.theta4)),5)>>}{<<n(p.AB + d.template.Re + p.BE*math.cos(math.radians(d.theta4)),5)>>} = <<n(d.theta3,3)>>^\circ.$$
Hence $\mathbf R_B = \mathbf R_1 + l_4e^{j\theta_4}$, i.e. B = (<<n(d.pos_B[0]*1000,2)>>, <<n(d.pos_B[1]*1000,2)>>) mm, and $\mathbf R_C = \mathbf R_2 + \overline{AC}e^{j(\theta_3-\beta)}$ with θ₃ − β = <<n(d.theta3 - p.beta_deg,3)>>°, i.e. C = (<<n(d.pos_C[0]*1000,2)>>, <<n(d.pos_C[1]*1000,2)>>) mm. Check: |B − A| = <<n(p.AB*1000,3)>> mm ✓.

**Part 3 – slider (point D).** The imaginary part of loop 2 is $y_C + l_5\sin\theta_5 = -H$ (the slider runs H *below* O, so $y_D = -H$):
$$\theta_5 = \arcsin\!\left(\frac{-H - y_C}{l_5}\right) = \arcsin\!\left(\frac{-<<n(p.H*1000,1)>> - <<n(d.pos_C[1]*1000,2)>>}{<<n(p.CD*1000,0)>>}\right) = <<n(d.theta5,3)>>^\circ,$$
$$S = x_D = x_C + l_5\cos\theta_5 = <<n(d.pos_C[0]*1000,2)>> + <<n(p.CD*1000,0)>>\cos(<<n(d.theta5,3)>>^\circ) = <<n(d.S*1000,2)>>\ \text{mm}.$$

**Mass-centre positions.** $\mathbf R_{G2} = \tfrac{l_2}{2}e^{j\theta_2}$, $\mathbf R_{G3} = \mathbf R_A + \tfrac{l_3}{2}e^{j(\theta_3-\beta/2)}$, $\mathbf R_{G4} = \mathbf R_1 + \tfrac{l_4}{2}e^{j\theta_4}$, $\mathbf R_{G5} = \mathbf R_C + \tfrac{l_5}{2}e^{j\theta_5}$, $\mathbf R_{G6} = \mathbf R_D$.

## 5.2 Velocity analysis

Differentiating loop 1 with respect to time (θ₁, l's constant):
$$j l_2\omega_2e^{j\theta_2} + j l_3\omega_3e^{j\theta_3} - j l_4\omega_4e^{j\theta_4} = 0 \quad\Leftrightarrow\quad \mathbf V_A + \mathbf V_{B/A} = \mathbf V_B.$$
Substituting $je^{j\theta} = -\sin\theta + j\cos\theta$ and separating real and imaginary parts gives two linear equations in ω₃ and ω₄:
$$\text{Re:}\quad -l_2\omega_2\sin\theta_2 - l_3\omega_3\sin\theta_3 + l_4\omega_4\sin\theta_4 = 0$$
$$\text{Im:}\quad\ \ \ l_2\omega_2\cos\theta_2 + l_3\omega_3\cos\theta_3 - l_4\omega_4\cos\theta_4 = 0$$
Multiplying Re by cos θ₄ and Im by sin θ₄ and adding eliminates ω₄; multiplying by cos θ₃ and sin θ₃ eliminates ω₃. With $\sin(x-y) = \sin x\cos y - \cos x\sin y$:
$$l_2\omega_2\sin(\theta_4-\theta_2) + l_3\omega_3\sin(\theta_4-\theta_3) = 0,\qquad l_2\omega_2\sin(\theta_3-\theta_2) + l_4\omega_4\sin(\theta_4-\theta_3) = 0,$$
so (Week 7):
$$\omega_3 = \frac{l_2\omega_2\sin(\theta_4-\theta_2)}{l_3\sin(\theta_3-\theta_4)} = \frac{<<n(p.OA,3)>>(<<n(p.omega2,4)>>)\sin(<<n(d.theta4-60,3)>>^\circ)}{<<n(p.AB,3)>>\sin(<<n(d.theta3-d.theta4,3)>>^\circ)} = <<n(d.omega3,4)>>\ \text{rad/s (CW)}$$
$$\omega_4 = \frac{l_2\omega_2\sin(\theta_2-\theta_3)}{l_4\sin(\theta_4-\theta_3)} = \frac{<<n(p.OA,3)>>(<<n(p.omega2,4)>>)\sin(<<n(60-d.theta3,3)>>^\circ)}{<<n(p.BE,3)>>\sin(<<n(d.theta4-d.theta3,3)>>^\circ)} = <<n(d.omega4,4)>>\ \text{rad/s (CCW)}$$
Point velocities, using $j e^{j\theta} = -\sin\theta + j\cos\theta$:
$$\mathbf V_A = l_2\omega_2(-\sin\theta_2 + j\cos\theta_2) = <<n(d.VA[0],4)>> + j\,<<n(d.VA[1],4)>>\ \text{m/s}$$
$$\mathbf V_B = l_4\omega_4(-\sin\theta_4 + j\cos\theta_4) = <<n(d.VB[0],4)>> + j\,(<<n(d.VB[1],4)>>)\ \text{m/s}$$
$$\mathbf V_C = \mathbf V_A + \overline{AC}\,\omega_3\,[-\sin(\theta_3-\beta) + j\cos(\theta_3-\beta)] = <<n(d.VC[0],4)>> + j\,<<n(d.VC[1],4)>>\ \text{m/s}$$
Differentiating loop 2, $\mathbf V_C + j l_5\omega_5 e^{j\theta_5} = \dot S$ (real, since the slider moves horizontally):
$$\text{Im:}\ V_{Cy} + l_5\omega_5\cos\theta_5 = 0\ \Rightarrow\ \omega_5 = \frac{-V_{Cy}}{l_5\cos\theta_5} = \frac{-<<n(d.VC[1],4)>>}{<<n(p.CD,3)>>\cos(<<n(d.theta5,3)>>^\circ)} = <<n(d.omega5,4)>>\ \text{rad/s (CW)}$$
$$\text{Re:}\ V_D = \dot S = V_{Cx} - l_5\omega_5\sin\theta_5 = <<n(d.VD[0],4)>>\ \text{m/s (to the left)}$$
Mass-centre velocities: $\mathbf V_{G3} = \mathbf V_A + j\tfrac{l_3}{2}\omega_3e^{j(\theta_3-\beta/2)} = <<n(d.VG3[0],4)>> + j\,<<n(d.VG3[1],4)>>$ m/s; $\mathbf V_{G5} = \mathbf V_C + j\tfrac{l_5}{2}\omega_5e^{j\theta_5} = <<n(d.VG5[0],4)>> + j\,<<n(d.VG5[1],4)>>$ m/s; $\mathbf V_{G2} = \tfrac12\mathbf V_A$, $\mathbf V_{G4} = \tfrac12\mathbf V_B$.

## 5.3 Acceleration analysis

Differentiating the velocity loop again:
$$(j\alpha_2 - \omega_2^2)l_2e^{j\theta_2} + (j\alpha_3 - \omega_3^2)l_3e^{j\theta_3} - (j\alpha_4-\omega_4^2)l_4e^{j\theta_4} = 0\quad\Leftrightarrow\quad \mathbf a_A + \mathbf a_{B/A} = \mathbf a_B .$$
Substituting $e^{j\theta} = \cos\theta + j\sin\theta$ and separating real and imaginary parts (with a = l₂, b = l₃, c = l₄):
$$\text{Re:}\quad -a\alpha_2\sin\theta_2 - a\omega_2^2\cos\theta_2 - b\alpha_3\sin\theta_3 - b\omega_3^2\cos\theta_3 + c\alpha_4\sin\theta_4 + c\omega_4^2\cos\theta_4 = 0$$
$$\text{Im:}\quad\ \ \ a\alpha_2\cos\theta_2 - a\omega_2^2\sin\theta_2 + b\alpha_3\cos\theta_3 - b\omega_3^2\sin\theta_3 - c\alpha_4\cos\theta_4 + c\omega_4^2\sin\theta_4 = 0$$
Moving the known terms to the right-hand side gives the linear system $A\alpha_4 - B\alpha_3 = C$ (Re) and $D\alpha_4 - E\alpha_3 = F$ (Im), with the Week 7 constants:
$$\begin{aligned}
A &= c\sin\theta_4 = <<n(d.consts.A,5)>>, & B &= b\sin\theta_3 = <<n(d.consts.B,5)>>,\\
D &= c\cos\theta_4 = <<n(d.consts.D,5)>>, & E &= b\cos\theta_3 = <<n(d.consts.E,5)>>,
\end{aligned}$$
$$C = a\alpha_2\sin\theta_2 + a\omega_2^2\cos\theta_2 + b\omega_3^2\cos\theta_3 - c\omega_4^2\cos\theta_4 = <<n(d.consts.C,3)>>,$$
$$F = a\alpha_2\cos\theta_2 - a\omega_2^2\sin\theta_2 - b\omega_3^2\sin\theta_3 + c\omega_4^2\sin\theta_4 = <<n(d.consts.F,3)>>,$$
Solving the 2 × 2 system by Cramer's rule:
$$\alpha_3 = \frac{CD - AF}{AE - BD} = <<n(d.alpha3,3)>>\ \text{rad/s}^2\ (\text{CCW}),$$
$$\alpha_4 = \frac{CE - BF}{AE - BD} = <<n(d.alpha4,3)>>\ \text{rad/s}^2\ (\text{CCW}).$$
Point accelerations:
$$\mathbf a_A = (j\alpha_2-\omega_2^2)l_2e^{j\theta_2} = <<n(d.aA[0],3)>> + j(<<n(d.aA[1],3)>>)\ \text{m/s}^2$$
$$\mathbf a_B = (j\alpha_4-\omega_4^2)l_4e^{j\theta_4} = <<n(d.aB[0],3)>> + j(<<n(d.aB[1],3)>>)\ \text{m/s}^2$$
$$\mathbf a_C = \mathbf a_A + (j\alpha_3-\omega_3^2)\overline{AC}\,e^{j(\theta_3-\beta)} = <<n(d.aC[0],3)>> + j(<<n(d.aC[1],3)>>)\ \text{m/s}^2$$
Differentiating loop 2 again, $\mathbf a_C + (j\alpha_5-\omega_5^2)l_5e^{j\theta_5} = \ddot S$ (real). The imaginary part is $a_{Cy} + l_5\alpha_5\cos\theta_5 - l_5\omega_5^2\sin\theta_5 = 0$ and the real part gives $\ddot S$:
$$\alpha_5 = \frac{l_5\omega_5^2\sin\theta_5 - a_{Cy}}{l_5\cos\theta_5} = <<n(d.alpha5,3)>>\ \text{rad/s}^2\ (\text{CCW}),$$
$$a_D = \ddot S = a_{Cx} - l_5\alpha_5\sin\theta_5 - l_5\omega_5^2\cos\theta_5 = <<n(d.aD[0],3)>>\ \text{m/s}^2 .$$
Mass-centre accelerations, used in Section 6:

