# 6. Dynamic Force Analysis — Matrix Method (θ₂ = 60°) {#sec6}

**Assumptions.** Rigid links; frictionless pins; constant crank speed (α₂ = 0); no external load on the slider; gravity neglected (at θ₂ = 60° the inertia forces are <<n(gmin,0)>>–<<n(gmax,0)>> times the link weights; the effect of gravity is quantified in Section 7). The slider guide is frictionless in the baseline solution. Coulomb friction μ = 0.3, as in the lecture help example, is solved as a variant.

**Notation (Week 9).** $\mathbf F_{ij}$ is the force of link *i* on link *j*, and $\mathbf F_{ji} = -\mathbf F_{ij}$. $\mathbf R_{ij}$ is the vector from the mass centre $G_j$ to the joint where $\mathbf F_{ij}$ acts. The cross product of two planar vectors is $\mathbf R\times\mathbf F = R_xF_y - R_yF_x$.

