**Interpretation.** The ground pushes the slider *downwards* (F₁₆y = <<n(F0.F16y,1)>> N): the connecting rod pulls the slider up against the top face of the guide. The crank bearing O carries the largest load: $\mathbf F_{12} = m_2\mathbf a_{G2} - \mathbf F_{32}$, i.e. it reacts the <<n(pins[1].m,0)>> N force coming from the coupler at A plus the crank's own inertia force (<<n(p.masses.m2*mag(d.aG2),0)>> N).

**Checks.**

- *Power (virtual work) balance:* $T_{12}\,\omega_2 = \sum (m_i\mathbf a_{Gi}\cdot\mathbf V_{Gi} + I_i\alpha_i\omega_i)$ gives T₁₂ = <<n(s6.checks.T12_power,4)>> N·m, identical to the matrix solution.
- *Sum of ground reactions:* $\mathbf F_{12} + \mathbf F_{14} + \mathbf F_{16} = \sum m_i\mathbf a_{Gi}$ is satisfied to round-off.
- *Independent solver:* the multibody model of Section 7 returns the same pin forces from its Lagrange multipliers (difference < 10⁻⁷ N).
- *Lecture cross-check:* the Week 9 "Help for 6-bar linkage" slide uses the same OA = 0.18 m and n₂ = 440 rpm. Its link-2 numbers, $a_{G2}$ = 191.08 m/s² at 240°, $R_{12}$ = (−0.045, −0.078) m and $R_{32}$ = (0.045, 0.078) m, are identical to Tables 13 and 17, and its equation (3) is the same as row (3) of Table 18.
- *Sensitivity to the G₃ location:* placing G₃ at the triangle centroid (Table 3 note) instead of on the bisector changes $a_{G3}$ by <<n(g3s.aG3,1)>> %, T₁₂ by <<n(g3s.T12,1)>> %, |F₁₂| by <<n(g3s.F12,1)>> %, |F₃₂| by <<n(g3s.F32,1)>> % and |F₄₃| by <<n(g3s.F43,1)>> %. Forces at C, D and the slider are unchanged, because links 5 and 6 do not see link 3's mass distribution.

**With Coulomb friction at the slider (μ = 0.3).** Friction opposes sliding: $F_{16x} = -\mu\,|F_{16y}|\,\operatorname{sign}(V_D)$. The matrix needs the linear form $F_{16x} = \mu' F_{16y}$, so $\mu' = -\mu\,\operatorname{sign}(V_D)\operatorname{sign}(F_{16y})$, found by solving once with μ′ = 0 to get the signs. Here V_D < 0 and F₁₆y < 0, so μ′ = <<n(F3.mu_eff,1)>> and F₁₆x = +<<n(F3.F16x,2)>> N (pushing the slider to the right, against its leftward motion). The normal force rises to <<n(abs(F3.F16y),1)>> N and the driving torque to <<n(F3.T12,2)>> N·m. The extra <<n(F3.T12-F0.T12,2)>> N·m equals the friction power $|F_{16x}V_D|/\omega_2$. The lecture help example writes $F_{16x} = 0.3F_{16y}$ with a fixed + sign. That is only correct for one combination of slider direction and contact face, so check the sign at every position.

# 7. Computational Model (Working-Model-equivalent Simulation) {#sec7}

**Model.** Working Model 2D (WM) is a Windows program that was not available in the environment where this solution was prepared. The rubric accepts "Working Model / MATLAB Simulink / Excel or any other software", so the same model was built from first principles as a planar multibody simulation in Python (`analysis/simulation.py`), exactly the way WM represents it:

- five rigid bodies (links 2–6), each described by the (x, y, φ) of its mass centre → 15 coordinates, with the masses and moments of inertia of Table 3;
- six pin joints (O, A, B, E, C, D), each 2 constraint equations; one keyed-slot joint (slider on y = −H, no rotation), 2 equations; one velocity motor at O (φ₂ = ω₂t), 1 equation → 15 constraints;
- equations of motion $M\ddot q + \Phi_q^T\lambda = Q$ with Baumgarte constraint stabilisation, integrated in time with an 8th-order Runge–Kutta solver (DOP853, tolerance 10⁻¹¹) from a roughly sketched initial configuration that the program "assembles" by Newton–Raphson, as WM does;
- joint forces, the slot reaction (side load) and the motor torque taken from the Lagrange multipliers, the equivalent of WM force meters.

This model does **not** use the closed-form loop equations of Section 5, so it is a genuinely independent check.

