- **Computational vs analytical:** identical to better than 10⁻⁷ in every kinematic and dynamic quantity (Table 22). Two completely different formulations agreeing this closely confirms both. The mean driving torque over a cycle is <<n(s7.summary.T12_mean,4)>> N·m, i.e. zero. This is correct physics: with no load and no friction, the energy the motor puts in during part of the cycle is returned during the rest.
- **Computational vs graphical:** within the drawing accuracy (Table 16), ≤ <<n(max_dg_vel,1)>> % for velocities and ≤ <<n(max_dg_acc,1)>> % for accelerations.
- **Gravity.** Working Model has gravity switched on by default. Including it changes T₁₂ by at most <<n(s7.summary.gravity_T12_maxdiff,2)>> N·m and the side load by at most <<n(s7.summary.gravity_F16y_maxdiff,1)>> N (at 60°: T₁₂ = <<n(s7.summary.at60.T12_gravity,2)>> N·m, F₁₆y = <<n(s7.summary.at60.F16y_gravity,1)>> N). Switch gravity **off** (World ▸ Gravity ▸ None) when comparing with the matrix method.
- **Friction.** With μ = 0.3 the torque peak rises to <<n(s7.summary.mu03_T12_max,1)>> N·m and the mean torque becomes <<n(s7.summary.mu03_T12_mean,2)>> N·m, the friction loss. The side load jumps when the slider reverses (θ₂ = <<n(th_DR,1)>>° and <<n(th_DL,1)>>°), because the friction force changes direction.
- **Expected WM differences.** A real WM run typically differs from the analytical values by 0.1–1 %. The reasons are its integrator and animation step, meter rounding, gravity being left on, or measuring at a body's mass centre instead of at the joint. The FAQ notes that simulated results about 50 % lower than the analytical ones are the typical symptom of measuring at the COM. Put point elements on the joints before measuring.

## 7.1 How to build this model in Working Model 2D

1. **Set up:** View ▸ Workspace: tick Grid Lines and X,Y Axes. View ▸ Numbers and Units: SI (degrees); More Choices ▸ Rot. Velocity = rev/min (to show rpm). World ▸ Gravity: None (for comparison with Section 6).
2. **Draw the bodies at θ₂ = 60°** using the coordinates below (origin at O). Draw links 2, 4 and 5 as thin rectangles (e.g. 10 mm wide) between their joint points, link 3 as a polygon A-B-C, and the slider as a small rectangle at D. The ground parts are anchored (anchor tool): pivot O, pivot E and the slider track at y = −H.
3. **Mass properties (Window ▸ Properties):** enter the masses and moments of inertia of Table 3. Link 3's centre of mass must be at G₃, not at the polygon centroid: in Window ▸ Geometry set the COM offset. At the drawn position, G₃ − centroid = (<<n(wm.at60.G3_minus_centroid[0]*1000,2)>>, <<n(wm.at60.G3_minus_centroid[1]*1000,2)>>) mm in global axes (convert to the body's own axes if the polygon was drawn rotated).
4. **Joints:** pin joints at O (link 2–ground), A (2–3), B (3–4), E (4–ground), C (3–5) and D (5–6); a keyed slot joint between the slider and the horizontal track. Select all links ▸ Object ▸ Do Not Collide (FAQ).
5. **Motor:** at O, type Velocity, <<n(p.n2_rpm,0)>> rpm (positive = anticlockwise).
6. **Frames:** World ▸ Accuracy ▸ Animation step = T/16 = 60/(<<n(p.n2_rpm,0)>> × 16) = <<n(wm.frame16,7)>> s. The FAQ notes that WM rounds this box to 3 decimals: here 0.009 s, which is <<n(p.omega2*0.009*180/math.pi,2)>>° per frame instead of 22.5°, so 16 frames overshoot one revolution by <<n(16*p.omega2*0.009*180/math.pi - 360,0)>>°. Treat the frames as a quick visual check only, and take the exact 16 positions from a crank-angle meter, or from Tables 4 and 20. World ▸ Pause Control: `frame() = 16`. Without rounding, frame k (k = 0 … 15) of a model drawn at 60° would be position k + 1. For the FAQ's six-frame method (model drawn at θ₂ = 0°), use T/6 = <<n(wm.frame6,6)>> s; WM rounds it to 0.023 s, so the first frame lands at <<n(p.omega2*0.023*180/math.pi,1)>>° rather than exactly 60°.
7. **Meters:** put Point elements (snapped, "X") on A, B, C, D and on each mass centre. Then use Measure ▸ Position / Velocity / Acceleration on the points, Measure ▸ Force on the pins and slot, and Measure ▸ Torque on the motor. Joint paths: select a point ▸ Appearance ▸ Track centre of mass.

