## 8.3 Analytical balancing strategy

A mechanism exerts no shaking force when the **total centre of mass of its moving links is stationary**, because $\mathbf F_s = -M_{tot}\,\mathbf a_{cm}$. The strategy below makes this as nearly true as possible using counterweights on the two links that are pivoted to the frame (crank 2 and rocker 4), since they are the only places where added mass can be supported by an existing bearing.

**Step 1 – Model link 5 by two point masses.** Replace link 5 by $m_5/2 = <<n(p.masses.m5/2,2)>>$ kg at C and $m_5/2$ at D. This has the same mass and centre of mass, so it gives exactly the same shaking force. Link 3 then carries $m_3' = m_3 + m_5/2 = <<n(s8.berkof.m3p,2)>>$ kg with its mass centre, in a frame fixed to link 3 (origin A, x along AB), at
$$g_3 = \frac{m_3\,\tfrac{l_3}{2}e^{-j\beta/2} + \tfrac{m_5}{2}\,\overline{AC}\,e^{-j\beta}}{m_3'} = <<n(s8.berkof.g3[0],4)>> <<"+" if s8.berkof.g3[1] >= 0 else "−">> j\,<<n(abs(s8.berkof.g3[1]),4)>>\ \text{m}.$$
The reciprocating mass at D is $m_{rec} = m_6 + m_5/2 = <<n(s8.m_rec,2)>>$ kg.

**Step 2 – Berkof–Lowen balance of the four-bar O-A-B-E.** Write the total mass moment of links 2, 3′ and 4 using body-fixed complex vectors ($P_2$ for link 2 about O, $P_4$ for link 4 about E):
$$M\mathbf r_{cm} = P_2e^{j\theta_2} + m_3'\left(l_2e^{j\theta_2} + g_3e^{j\theta_3}\right) + m_4\mathbf R_E + P_4e^{j\theta_4}.$$
Eliminate $e^{j\theta_3}$ with loop 1, $e^{j\theta_3} = (l_1e^{j\theta_1} + l_4e^{j\theta_4} - l_2e^{j\theta_2})/l_3$:
$$M\mathbf r_{cm} = \Big[P_2 + m_3'l_2\big(1 - \tfrac{g_3}{l_3}\big)\Big]e^{j\theta_2} + \Big[P_4 + m_3'\tfrac{g_3}{l_3}l_4\Big]e^{j\theta_4} + \text{constant}.$$
The centre of mass is stationary for every θ₂ if both brackets vanish:
$$P_{2,req} = -m_3'l_2\Big(1-\frac{g_3}{l_3}\Big) = <<n(s8.berkof.M2_req[0],4)>> <<"+" if s8.berkof.M2_req[1] >= 0 else "−">> j\,<<n(abs(s8.berkof.M2_req[1]),4)>>\ \text{kg}\cdot\text{m},$$
$$P_{4,req} = -m_3'\frac{g_3\,l_4}{l_3} = <<n(s8.berkof.M4_req[0],4)>> <<"+" if s8.berkof.M4_req[1] >= 0 else "−">> j\,<<n(abs(s8.berkof.M4_req[1]),4)>>\ \text{kg}\cdot\text{m}.$$
Subtracting what the links already have ($m_2l_2/2 = <<n(p.masses.m2*p.OA/2,3)>>$ kg·m and $m_4l_4/2 = <<n(p.masses.m4*p.BE/2,3)>>$ kg·m along the links) gives the counterweight mass moments
$$m_{c2}\,\mathbf u_2 = <<n(s8.berkof.P2[0],4)>> <<"+" if s8.berkof.P2[1] >= 0 else "−">> j\,<<n(abs(s8.berkof.P2[1]),4)>>\ \text{kg}\cdot\text{m}\ (|\,|= <<n((s8.berkof.P2[0]**2+s8.berkof.P2[1]**2)**0.5,3)>>),$$
$$m_{c4}\,\mathbf u_4 = <<n(s8.berkof.P4[0],4)>> <<"+" if s8.berkof.P4[1] >= 0 else "−">> j\,<<n(abs(s8.berkof.P4[1]),4)>>\ \text{kg}\cdot\text{m}\ (|\,|= <<n((s8.berkof.P4[0]**2+s8.berkof.P4[1]**2)**0.5,3)>>).$$
In practice: a <<n(s8.designs.berkof_lowen.m2c,2)>> kg mass at 120 mm on the crank, almost opposite A (angle <<n(ang(s8.designs.berkof_lowen.u2),1)>>° from OA), and a <<n(s8.designs.berkof_lowen.m4c,2)>> kg mass at 120 mm on an extension of the rocker beyond E (angle <<n(ang(s8.designs.berkof_lowen.u4),1)>>° from EB). A larger radius reduces the masses in proportion (m·r is what matters).

**Step 3 – The residual.** After Step 2, $\mathbf F_s = -m_{rec}\,a_D$, which is purely horizontal (verified numerically to 10⁻¹²). It cannot be cancelled by a rotating counterweight, which produces a force of constant magnitude rotating at crank speed. Such a counterweight can only remove the forward-rotating half of the first harmonic of $a_D$, and it trades horizontal for vertical force. The best amount is found by least squares (Step 4).

**Step 4 – Optimum.** Because the counterweights do not change the kinematics, their inertia forces are *linear* in the complex mass moments: $\mathbf F_s(\theta) = \mathbf F_{s0}(\theta) + P_2\,\omega_2^2e^{j\theta_2} - P_4\,(j\alpha_4-\omega_4^2)e^{j\theta_4}$. Minimising $\sum_\theta|\mathbf F_s|^2$ over one revolution is therefore a linear least-squares problem for P₂ and P₄, with optimum $P_2 = <<n(s8.optimal.P2[0],4)>> <<"+" if s8.optimal.P2[1] >= 0 else "−">> j\,<<n(abs(s8.optimal.P2[1]),4)>>$ and $P_4 = <<n(s8.optimal.P4[0],4)>> <<"+" if s8.optimal.P4[1] >= 0 else "−">> j\,<<n(abs(s8.optimal.P4[1]),4)>>$ kg·m.

::: {.small}
| Strategy | Counterweights (at 120 mm) | RMS $F_s$ (N) | Peak $F_s$ (N) | Peak $M_s$ (N·m) | Torque range (N·m) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
<% for name, stt, des in bal_rows %>
| <<name>> | << ("crank %s kg, rocker %s kg" % (n(des.m2c,2), n(des.m4c,2))) if des else "–" >> | <<n(stt.Fs_rms,0)>> | <<n(stt.Fs_max,0)>> | <<n(stt.Ms_max,0)>> | <<n(stt.T_min,0)>> … +<<n(stt.T_max,0)>> |
