## 8.4 Discussion

- The **Berkof–Lowen** counterweights remove all of the shaking force except the slider's reciprocating part. The RMS falls by <<n(-pct(s8.stats.berkof_lowen.Fs_rms, s8.stats.unbalanced.Fs_rms),0)>> % and the peak by <<n(-pct(s8.stats.berkof_lowen.Fs_max, s8.stats.unbalanced.Fs_max),0)>> %, and the remaining force is purely horizontal, which is easier to absorb in the machine frame. The shaking moment also falls, by <<n(-pct(s8.stats.berkof_lowen.Ms_max, s8.stats.unbalanced.Ms_max),0)>> % in peak.
- The **least-squares optimum** cuts the RMS shaking force by <<n(-pct(s8.stats.optimal_lsq.Fs_rms, s8.stats.unbalanced.Fs_rms),0)>> %. It does so by over-balancing the crank and rocker slightly, so that part of the reciprocating force is cancelled at the cost of a small vertical component.
- **Costs.** About <<n(s8.designs.optimal_lsq.m2c + s8.designs.optimal_lsq.m4c,1)>> kg of counterweights is added to a mechanism whose moving links weigh <<n(p.masses.m2+p.masses.m3+p.masses.m4+p.masses.m5+p.masses.m6,1)>> kg. The driving-torque range grows from ±<<n(s8.stats.unbalanced.T_max,0)>> to <<n(s8.stats.optimal_lsq.T_min,0)>> … +<<n(s8.stats.optimal_lsq.T_max,0)>> N·m, and the bearings at O and E carry the counterweight inertia. A flywheel on the crank shaft is recommended to keep the speed constant, and the bearings should be sized for the larger loads.
- **Complete balancing** would also require cancelling the reciprocating force. One option is a mirror-image second slider driven 180° out of phase from the same crank (an opposed arrangement). Another is a dedicated balancing slider. A counter-rotating inertia geared to the crank can remove the shaking moment. These add complexity, and are worthwhile only if the residual of about <<n(s8.stats.optimal_lsq.Fs_max,0)>> N peak is still too large for the application.
- **Recommended strategy:** fit Berkof–Lowen counterweights on links 2 and 4, then trim the crank counterweight towards the least-squares value, plus a flywheel. This removes most of the shaking force with two simple parts and keeps the remaining force horizontal.

# Conclusions {#conclusions}

1. The selected dimensions (AB = <<n(p.AB*1000,0)>> mm, BE = <<n(p.BE*1000,0)>> mm) satisfy the course selection rules and Grashof's criterion. The mechanism is a crank–rocker driving a slider through a <<n(stroke*1000,1)>> mm stroke with a quick-return ratio of <<n(Q,2)>>, and the transmission angle never falls below <<n(R.transmission.fourbar_min,1)>>°.
2. At θ₂ = 60° the slider moves left at <<n(mag(d.VD),3)>> m/s and is still speeding up (a_D = <<n(mag(d.aD),1)>> m/s² to the left), and the links' angular velocities are ω₃ = <<n(abs(d.omega3),2)>> rad/s CW, ω₄ = <<n(abs(d.omega4),2)>> rad/s CCW and ω₅ = <<n(abs(d.omega5),2)>> rad/s CW. The angular accelerations are α₃ = <<n(d.alpha3,1)>>, α₄ = <<n(d.alpha4,1)>> and α₅ = <<n(d.alpha5,1)>> rad/s², all CCW.
3. The four independent methods (graphical polygons, instantaneous centres, analytical vector loops and the computational model) agree, within <<n(max_dg_vel,1)>> % (velocity) and <<n(max_dg_acc,1)>> % (acceleration) for drawings read at ruler precision, and to 10⁻⁷ between the analytical and computational models.
4. At θ₂ = 60° the matrix method gives a driving torque of <<n(F0.T12,1)>> N·m and a largest pin force of <<n(pins[0].m,0)>> N at the crank bearing. Over a revolution the torque swings ±<<n(s7.summary.T12_max,0)>> N·m and the crank bearing peaks at <<n(s7.summary.pin_max.F12,0)>> N.
5. The unbalanced shaking force (up to <<n(s8.stats.unbalanced.Fs_max,0)>> N) can be reduced by <<n(-pct(s8.stats.optimal_lsq.Fs_rms, s8.stats.unbalanced.Fs_rms),0)>> % (RMS) with two counterweights. The residual reciprocating force of the slider can only be removed with an opposed or balancing slider.

