# Appendix A – Notes on the lecture examples {#appendixA}

While checking this solution against the lecture material, three slips were found in the worked examples. They are listed here so that they do not cause confusion; the *methods and formulas* in the notes are correct.

1. **Week 7, four-bar position example** (a = 40, b = 120, c = 80, d = 100 mm, θ₂ = 40°). The handwritten result gives θ₄(open) = 98° and θ₄(crossed) = −57°. Substituting back into the loop shows |AB| = 79 mm for θ₄ = 98°, not 120 mm. The slip is the extra minus sign written in front of 2 tan⁻¹(…). Without it, the two roots of the same quadratic are θ₄ = −98.0° and θ₄ = **+57.3°**, and the root that pairs with θ₃ = 20.2° (the configuration used in the velocity and acceleration examples) is θ₄ = 57.3°; the other pair is θ₃ = −60.9°, θ₄ = −98.0°.
2. **Week 7, velocity example** (ω₂ = 25 rad/s). ω₄ is evaluated with sin(40° − 23.3°) instead of sin(40° − 20.3°). The correct value is ω₄ = **7.00 rad/s**, not 5.98 rad/s. The acceleration example inherits this (and uses 45 instead of a = 40 in F), so it should read α₃ ≈ **296 rad/s²** and α₄ ≈ **470 rad/s²**. Both were confirmed by numerical differentiation.
3. **Week 7, template Part 3 (slider).** The template writes $Y_C + l_5\sin\theta_5 = H$ but also $Y_D = -H$ for a slider below O. For the handout geometry, use $Y_C + l_5\sin\theta_5 = -H$, i.e. $\theta_5 = \arcsin[-(H+Y_C)/l_5]$ (Section 5.1).

**Always check a computed configuration by substituting it back into the loop equations.**

